 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the navigation law, in relation to requiring working marine\ncarbon monoxide detectors to be carried on certain boats",
    "basePrintNo": "S1270",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S1270-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §40, Nav L",
    "lawSection": "Navigation Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S1270\n\nSPONSOR: BROOKS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the navigation law, in relation to requiring working\nmarine carbon monoxide detectors to be carried on certain boats\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to prospectively require that carbon monox-\nide detectors be installed onboard any boat that includes a cabin before\nits sale.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. amends section 40 of the navigation law by creating a new\nsubdivision 9-a which provides a requirement that new vessels for sale\ninclude a carbon monoxide detector. This section only applies to vessels\nthat include an enclosed sleeping accommodation, galley with sink, or\nhead compartment. This section further includes old vessels for sale\nthat have undergone significant renovations, and includes the above\nmentioned parameters.\n\nSection 2. is the enacting clause.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCarbon monoxide, which is often called a silent killer, is an odorless,\ncolorless gas that is produced whenever a fuel such as gasoline is\nburned.\n\nEach year carbon monoxide poisoning is responsible for some 500 deaths\nand 15,000 visits to the emergency room.\n\nBoat engines, which produce Carbon Monoxide, are often housed internally\nin vessels that include cabin space. This creates a very real possibil-\nity of carbon monoxide building up to harmful levels inside the cabin,\nputting many New York boat owners needlessly at risk. Carbon monoxide\ndetectors are an affordable and reliable solution to providing an effec-\ntive alert system in boat cabins.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the state\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the one hundred and eightieth day after it\nshall become law.\n",
